{
  "headline": "Voters scheduled to cast ballots in Toronto's 2026 municipal election",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "According to the City of Toronto, voters are scheduled to head to the polls on a Monday for the 2026 municipal election. On election day, voting places will be open from 10 a.m. to 8 p.m. While voting places close at 8 p.m., any voter in line by that time will be permitted to vote. To avoid lineups, the city suggests voters consider voting during off-peak hours between 11 a.m. and 3 p.m."
    },
    {
      "text": "Eligible voters must vote at their assigned voting place as indicated on their Voter Information Card (VIC) or the Toronto Elections webpage. To be eligible, a person must vote in the ward where they live, and may only vote once regardless of how many properties they own or rent in Toronto. To vote in person, individuals must show one piece of acceptable identification that includes their name and a qualifying Toronto address; photo identification is not required. The city encourages voters to bring their paper or digital VIC to facilitate a faster process. Voters on the voters' list can access their digital VIC and a list of acceptable identification at www.toronto.ca/Elections."
    },
    {
      "text": "Voters may bring a friend, family member, caregiver, or other support person to assist them at the voting place. Individuals assisting a voter behind the voting screen are required to complete a declaration first. Alternatively, an election official can provide assistance to voters with accessibility needs."
    }
  ]
}

